# Male frustrated -> bar wanders -> female reads his pattern
# The core intended interaction loop, continuing from startup.timeline once one drive crosses the frustration threshold. This is the first scenario that exercises Female.Search since its crash was fixed (see SCENARIOS.md 2.1) - nothing currently closes the loop by satisfying the matched drive (SCENARIOS.md 7.1), so this timeline ends at "pattern detected", not "male satisfied".

0 male1's O drive crosses value=75; which_is_frustated() now returns ("O",) (hardware/male/drives/__init__.py)
0 male1.loop() sees not is_satisfied() and search not started -> starts male1.search (hardware/male/__init__.py)
0 male1.search.setup() starts male1.search.blink: ring set to white, begins rotating the 10-bit ("O",) pattern (1,1,0,0,0,0,0,1,1,1) on/off at 0.5s/bit
5 male1 sways between his min/max position on every tick he isn't already moving, ring blinking the same 5s-repeating pattern throughout
0 bar.loop() sees a male searching and starts bar.search: the bar begins a full-range wander, independent of which female is actually relevant (hardware/bar/__init__.py)
8 ~ bar wanders past female1's interaction origin at some point during its search sway - exact timing depends on where in its cycle the bar already was
0 Meanwhile female1's own drives have also been climbing since startup; her O drive crosses back under the satisfaction limit and she becomes unsatisfied
0 female1.loop() starts female1.search: search.setup() starts female1.search.read_pattern
5.5 female1 sways min/max (same toggle_position() pattern as male1) while read_pattern buffers her light sensor readings roughly every 0.01s, waiting for 5s of buffered span before it can attempt a match
0 ~ bar happens to be positioned so male1 is facing female1 at their interaction origin - in the live installation this alignment is opportunistic, not coordinated by any code
2 read_pattern._try_match() tries all 10 sub-step offsets x all 10 rotations of every reference pattern against the buffered samples
0 Match found: read_pattern.last_match = ("male1", ("O",)); logged as "Pattern detected: male1 drive=('O',)" (throttled to at most once per 2s)
0 Nothing currently acts on this match - male1's O drive keeps counting, female1 keeps swaying and re-matching periodically. This is where the intended loop currently ends (SCENARIOS.md 7.1, "suggested next steps" #3)
